y options: Fold (-1) or Call All-in (-2)\n\n**Hand Strength Analysis:**\nK7 off-suit is a marginal hand - it's below average for heads-up play. It's not a premium hand like pocket pairs, AK, AQ, or even suited connectors. Against a random hand, K7o has roughly 50-55% equity, but against an all-in range, my opponent likely has either:\n1. A premium hand (AA, KK, QQ, JJ, AK, AQ) - I'm likely dominated\n2. A bluff/semi-bluff with any two cards\n\n**Opponent's All-in Pre-flop:**\nAn immediate all-in pre-flop is a very aggressive move. This could indicate:\n- A very strong hand (trying to protect it or get maximum value)\n- A bluff (trying to steal the blinds/pot immediately)\n- A strategic pattern they're establishing\n\n**Risk-Reward Analysis:**\n- If I fold: I lose the small blind (100 chips), keeping 19,900 chips\n- If I call and win: I double up to ~40,000 chips\n- If I call and lose: I'm back to starting 20,000 chips for the next hand\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- I'm currently ahead by 50 chips cumulatively (slight lead)\n- It's only hand 9 of 20 - plenty of hands remaining\n- K7o is not strong enough to risk everything on\n- The cumulative chips are independent, so preserving my current stack matters for this hand only\n\nReason:\n\nK7 off-suit is simply too weak to call an all-in bet pre-flop. While my opponent could be bluffing, the risk-reward doesn't justify calling with such a marginal hand. I'm likely either dominated by a better King (KQ, KJ, K10) or facing a pocket pair. Even in the best-case scenario where they're bluffing with a worse hand, I'm not a significant favorite. With 11 more hands to play and only a small blind to lose, folding is the prudent choice to preserve my stack and wait for a better spot.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
