aluation:\nLet me analyze this situation on the Turn (4th community card):\n\n**My Hand:** 5♥, K♦\n**Board:** 9♣, 9♠, K♥, 3♥\n**My Best Hand:** Two Pair (Kings and Nines) with K♦, K♥, 9♣, 9♠, 5♥\n\n**Key Observations:**\n1. I have a strong two pair (Kings over Nines)\n2. My opponent only called my 400 raise on the flop, showing moderate strength\n3. There's one card to come (the River)\n4. I'm currently behind in the match (-50 chips cumulatively, with 19,500 vs likely 20,500 for opponent)\n\n**Possible Opponent Hands:**\n- **Dangerous:** Trip 9s (if they have a 9), Full House (unlikely given their passive play), pocket pair that makes a full house\n- **Likely:** Weaker two pair, one pair (Aces, Queens, Jacks), drawing to flush (needs another heart)\n- **Less likely:** Complete bluff (they called my raise)\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- Opponent only called my flop raise rather than re-raising, suggesting they don't have trips or better\n- If they had a 9, they likely would have raised for value\n- The flush draw is present (2 hearts on board), but they'd need exactly 2 hearts in hand\n- I should continue applying pressure to extract value or make worse hands fold\n\n**Options:**\n1. **Check:** Safe but passive; gives free card and loses value from worse hands\n2. **Small raise (100-500):** Continues building pot, but may not get calls from worse hands\n3. **Medium raise (800-1200):** Good value bet that worse two pairs/overpairs might call\n4. **All-in:** Too aggressive; folds out everything worse, only called by better hands\n\nReason:\nI'll bet around 800-1000 to extract value from weaker Kings, pocket pairs (QQ, JJ, TT), or weaker two pairs. This sizing allows them to call with worse hands while protecting against the flush draw. If they have trips or better, they'll raise and I can reassess. Given I'm behind in the match, I need to win chips, and my two pair is strong enough to bet for value.\n\nAnswer: 900"}
